            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">

                <h6 class="fw-bold">How it works</h6>
                <p>
                    Connect the <strong>IF output (rear-panel RCA socket)</strong> of your FTdx101 to the
                    antenna input of your SDR receiver using a coax cable with an RCA-to-SMA adapter.
                    The app tunes the SDR to the radio's IF frequency (9 MHz for the FTdx101 series) and
                    displays the surrounding band as a live spectrum waterfall.
                </p>

                <h6 class="fw-bold mt-3">Supported SDR hardware</h6>
                <p>Support for the following devices is built in — no additional software installation required:</p>
                <table class="table table-sm table-bordered">
                    <thead class="table-dark">
                        <tr><th>Device</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>RTL-SDR</strong></td>
                            <td>Any RTL2832U-based dongle (RTL-SDR Blog v3/v4, Nooelec, etc.). Plug in and scan.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Airspy</strong></td>
                            <td>Airspy Mini, Airspy R2. Plug in and scan.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>HackRF One</strong></td>
                            <td>Plug in and scan.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>SDRplay RSP1 / RSP1A / RSP1B / RSP2 / RSPduo / RSPdx</strong></td>
                            <td>Requires the SDRplay API — see below.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h6 class="fw-bold mt-3"><i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle-fill text-warning me-1"></i>SDRplay — additional step required</h6>
                <p>
                    SDRplay devices use a background Windows service provided by SDRplay Ltd.
                    You must install the <strong>SDRplay API</strong> before the device will be detected:
                </p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        Go to <a href="https://www.sdrplay.com/downloads/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">www.sdrplay.com/downloads/</a>
                        and download the latest <strong>SDRplay API/Hardware Driver</strong> for Windows.
                    </li>
                    <li>Run the installer. It installs the USB driver and starts the <em>SDRplay API Service</em>.</li>
                    <li>Plug in your RSP device, then click <strong>Scan</strong> on this page.</li>
                </ol>
                <p class="text-muted small">
                    Only one application can use the SDRplay API at a time.
                    If SDR Console or another SDR app is open, close it before scanning.
                </p>

                <h6 class="fw-bold mt-3">Troubleshooting</h6>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>No devices found after scan</strong> — check the device is plugged in and that Windows Device Manager shows it without a yellow warning icon.</li>
                    <li><strong>SDRplay not detected</strong> — open <em>services.msc</em> and confirm <em>SDRplay API Service</em> is running.</li>
                    <li><strong>Spectrum looks wrong / distorted</strong> — check your IF frequency setting matches the radio (FTdx101: 9,000,000 Hz).</li>
